<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Supplementary details about the methods</h3>

</section>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Implementation details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models are queried via Amazon Bedrock API at temperature <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with a maximum of 1 output tokens to enforce constrained responses, with the
target answer parsed from the first valid token. No model-specific
tuning, system prompt modifications, or few-shot examples are
applied in the baseline evaluation all models receive the
same prompt format across all conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Presupposition taxonomy</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ A.2 Presupposition taxonomy ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SWAY: A Counterfactual Computational Linguistic Approach to Measuring and Mitigating Sycophancy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,  <a href="#A1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ A.2 Presupposition taxonomy ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SWAY: A Counterfactual Computational Linguistic Approach to Measuring and Mitigating Sycophancy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and
Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.2 Presupposition taxonomy ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SWAY: A Counterfactual Computational Linguistic Approach to Measuring and Mitigating Sycophancy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> present the full presupposition
taxonomy used across all three datasets. Each condition is
defined by a tuple of clause type, construction, commitment
level, and polarity. Epistemic commitment levels follow
Rubin’s continuum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rubin, 2010</a>)</cite>: low (possibility),
medium (probability), and high (certainty). The certainty
expressions used as stance markers, including hedges such as
<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maybe</span> and <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">probably</span>, and boosters such as
<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">certainly</span> correspond to well-studied epistemic
markers in natural language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Stengel-Eskin, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rubin, 2010</a>)</cite>. Tagged declaratives are formed by appending
a reverse-polarity tag question to a declarative base
, imperative constructions follow the commitment-bearing
imperative form
, and preposed negation questions (PrepNegQ) are formed by
fronting a negated auxiliary
. Epistemic markers are shown in <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>. <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PP^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PP^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is
formed by negating the stance marker in each case.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Presupposition taxonomy - AITA. Base prompt:
<span id="A1.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“AITA for </span>{<span id="A1.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">…</span>}<span id="A1.T1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">?”</span> Epistemic markers in <span id="A1.T1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
