<section id="A1.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.8 </span>Statistical analysis: paired <math id="A1.SS8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ A.8 Statistical analysis: paired 𝑡-test results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SWAY: A Counterfactual Computational Linguistic Approach to Measuring and Mitigating Sycophancy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.8 Statistical analysis: paired 𝑡-test results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SWAY: A Counterfactual Computational Linguistic Approach to Measuring and Mitigating Sycophancy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, and
<a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.8 Statistical analysis: paired 𝑡-test results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SWAY: A Counterfactual Computational Linguistic Approach to Measuring and Mitigating Sycophancy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> report paired <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test results comparing
adjacent commitment levels; low vs. medium (L<math id="A1.SS8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>M) and
medium vs. high (M<math id="A1.SS8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>H) for each model and clause type
across all three datasets. For each pair of adjacent commitment
levels, we test whether the difference in <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> between the two
levels is significantly different from zero, using the 500 base
prompts as paired observations. A negative <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-statistic
indicates that higher commitment produces greater sycophancy
i.e. <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> increases as commitment increases. A positive
<math id="A1.SS8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-statistic indicates a decline in <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> at the higher
commitment level. Entries marked with <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\!\!-\!\!-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.410em;" width="0.410em"><mo>−</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.410em;" width="0.410em"><mo>−</mo></mpadded><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\!\!-\!\!-</annotation></semantics></math> correspond
to comparisons that cannot be computed: Rising Imperative only
appears at low commitment, so no L<math id="A1.SS8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>M or M<math id="A1.SS8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>H comparison
exists for that construction; similarly, Neutral Polar Question
only appears at low commitment, leaving PrepNegQ comparisons
at medium and high only. <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{*}p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{*}p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{**}p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>p</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{**}p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.SS8.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{***}p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{***}p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the majority of comparisons are statistically
significant, particularly at the L<math id="A1.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>M transition under
plain declaratives, where negative <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-statistics confirm
that moving from possibility to probability-level commitment
reliably increases sycophancy across most models and datasets.
The M<math id="A1.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>H transition is more variable, with some models
showing continued increases and others plateauing or reversing,
consistent with the saturation effect observed for declarative
constructions in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Effect of linguistic commitment and clause type ‣ 4 Results ‣ SWAY: A Counterfactual Computational Linguistic Approach to Measuring and Mitigating Sycophancy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
Mistral
entries are undefined due to near-zero variance in responses
across bootstrap samples, the model responds with the same
answer for the overwhelming majority of prompts in these
conditions, causing the <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test to be undefined. This reflects
an extreme response bias rather than a missing data issue.</p>
</div>
